updated: 2026-10-06T19:49:26Z
---

`bin/mborg:103-107`:

```
sub pp {
    require Data::Printer;
    shift if ref $_[0] eq 'WWW::MailboxOrg';
    Data::Printer->new( indent => 2, colored => 1 )->p(@_);
}
```

`Data::Printer` has no constructor (the object interface is `Data::Printer::Object`; the public one is the exported `p`/`np`):

```
perl -e 'require Data::Printer; Data::Printer->new( indent => 2 )'
# Can't locate object method "new" via package "Data::Printer"   (Data::Printer 1.002001)
```

`pp(` is the output path of all 45 call sites in the script, so each command performs its API call (including the mutating ones -- `account add`, `domain del`, ...) and then dies instead of showing the result. On a clean install it dies one line earlier, because `Data::Printer` is not in `cpanfile` at all. No test under `t/` runs the script (`grep -rl mborg t` is empty), which is why this went unnoticed.

Done when: `pp` uses a real interface (`Data::Printer::np($data, indent => 2, colored => 1)` and `print`, or JSON::MaybeXS pretty output and no extra dependency), whatever it uses is declared in `cpanfile`, and a test at least compiles `bin/mborg` (`perl -c`) and exercises `pp` with a hashref.

